WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:15.219
Last time on True Fear, I showed you the ghost
hand in our photo.

00:00:15.219 --> 00:00:17.619
Seriously, look right here, it's actually
there!

00:00:17.619 --> 00:00:21.550
So if you haven't seen that episode and want
to hear more about that… pop on over there.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:26.849
But on today's episode, it's Independence
​Day here in America, the land of the…

00:00:26.849 --> 00:00:28.150
Americans.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:33.140
And if you're part of the 32.1% of my audience
that is not from USA, then perhaps you've

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.579
heard of the 1996 blockbuster starring Will
Smith.

00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:40.440
If you haven't, I highly recommend that you
give that one a watch, but I can't say the

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:42.719
same for the sequel that came out 20 years
later.

00:00:42.719 --> 00:00:47.309
In fact, the only entertaining part of that
moviegoing experience was what went down in

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:51.000
the theater that evening.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.180
So being fans of the original, Claude Gnome
and I made plans to go see this new Independence

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:56.180
Day movie.

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:59.570
It wasn't like right when the movie came out
or anything and I think we saw it late on

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.440
a weekday, so there were some people in the
theater, but it wasn't packed.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:06.290
There was also some remodeling going on in
that theater at the time, so I think that

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.140
also helped keep the crowds down a bit.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:12.770
At this particular cinema, they have big recliners,
so it's not super high capacity anyways.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.810
We were in the second row from the back, basically
in the middle of the screen.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:19.800
There were some couples scattered around in
front of us, a family who was in our row all

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:24.479
the way to the left, one guy in the row behind
us in the right corner, and a group of people

00:01:24.479 --> 00:01:29.890
who were also in that back row over in the
left corner.

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:34.180
The movie starts and about five or ten minutes
in, the man sitting by himself in the back-right

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.540
starts talking on the phone.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.580
I don't remember what he was talking about,
but the only reason I didn't say something

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.840
right away was that there wasn't anything
interesting going on in the movie, this guy

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:45.880
was just slightly more entertaining than what
was happening on the screen.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.290
I also figured that he'd be off the phone
soon, before we got to any worthwhile scenes,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.430
so I just kind of tuned it out for a while.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:54.100
Let's just say that I'm glad that I did.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.760
A couple minutes later, the guy is STILL blabbering,
and that's when I've decided that enough is

00:01:58.760 --> 00:01:59.760
enough.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.270
I’m going to get up and call this guy out
and tell him, “hey, shut up, we’re trying

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:03.270
to watch the movie.”

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:07.700
Then literally as I turn to go confront this
guy, I hear someone from across the theater

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:08.750
yell out at him.

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.970
The first thing the phone guy does is make
his way across the row over to the people

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:12.970
who yelled at him.

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:16.000
My first impression was that they actually
knew each other, like they were supposed to

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.900
meet there or something and the phone guy
was going over there to say hi.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.250
And then even when they started yelling at
each other, I was just thinking that it was

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:29.620
the type of thing where they were buddies
and they were just giving each other a hard

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:30.620
time.

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:33.099
What happened next was what started to make
me get worried.

00:02:33.099 --> 00:02:36.660
First, the yelling escalates, and then the
guys start pushing each other around.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:40.430
Then the woman from the family in front of
them gets up and starts hollering at them,

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.950
telling them to “knock it off” and “stop
that right now”.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.830
This causes the group that was fighting in
the back to start getting into with her and

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:47.870
her husband.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:50.620
At this point the commotion is completely
drowning out the movie.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.260
I think we probably would have fared fine
in a brawl, but the scary part was when I

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:58.000
turned and saw one of the couples in front
of us quickly get up and speed out of the

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.580
theater, because this made me think that they
had some reason to be afraid, despite being

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.129
pretty far away from where the fight was taking
place.

00:03:04.129 --> 00:03:07.680
The first thing that came to my head is that
they were trying to escape because someone

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.599
up there had a gun, and after what took place
in Colorado in 2012, and after the increasing

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:16.099
amount of shootings taking place in this country
since that incident, the idea of something

00:03:16.099 --> 00:03:18.840
happening here was actually pretty scary.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:23.080
These people seemed like pretty shady characters
as well, and if someone is dumb enough to

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.209
start a fight in a movie theater, who knows
if they are also stupid enough to carry a

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:27.450
weapon into one.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:31.239
And I think part of what started to make that
possibility scary as the situation continued

00:03:31.239 --> 00:03:36.349
to escalate is the way that they were acting,
which was clearly outside of the social normal,

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:40.879
and the way they were dressed, which was,
without a more eloquent way of explaining:

00:03:40.879 --> 00:03:42.299
they were lookin’ pretty thug.

00:03:42.299 --> 00:03:46.830
And I'm not saying that everyone who follows
those fashion trends is a dangerous criminal

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:47.830
or anything.

00:03:47.830 --> 00:03:51.010
I think that makes about as much sense as
when people see me always wearing black and

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:52.840
assume that means I'm sad or something.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.610
But in the end I think it's just something
that contributed to us feeling nervous about

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:57.940
the situation.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:00.980
And Claude Gnome would later tell me that
he felt the way about it.

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:06.370
However, we paid like 14 bucks to get into
that movie, and that's before you even factor

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:11.120
in the cost of parking, so we were gonna stick
that one out no matter how bad the movie was,

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:16.070
no matter how obnoxious the other audience
members were, no matter how beaten and bruised

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:20.620
we were at risk of becoming by entering this
brawl, and no matter how likely it was that

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:25.270
we would be shot and killed before this movie
let out, so we stood our ground.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:29.540
So eventually the lady who was yelling at
them to stop… and hold on, can we pause

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:30.540
for a second?

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.300
Why is it that whenever you see videos of
a fight, or just some kind of commotion in

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:37.380
general that there always some annoying lady
yelling out about how wrong this is?

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:38.540
How disgusted she is?

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.000
How we all just need to stop and think of
the children?

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.350
It just seems like that lady is always there.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.350
Ok…

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:45.350
Time in.

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:47.390
So the lady takes her family and storms out
the cinema.

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:50.790
I'm not sure if she threatened to report them
to all to the management or something, as

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:54.360
I couldn't really make out all that was being
said, but this is what kind of triggered them

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.690
to start moving the fight towards the exits,
and they ended up duking it out down the aisle

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:02.290
until eventually one of them made a run for
it across the width of the theater and out

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:07.320
of our auditorium, so that was kind of a relief,
but at the same time it was the end of that

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:08.880
evening's entertainment…

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.000
Or so we thought…

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:15.890
You would probably expect that after being
in an incident like that, the hoodlums would

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:18.080
book it, and get out of there before the police
showed up.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:22.540
I'm not exactly sure how long had passed before
the three who started the confrontation come

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:24.640
marching back into the theater.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.520
Not long after that, the original phone guy
comes back in and stands at the exit, just

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:29.640
staring into the auditorium.

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:32.190
That's where I was thinking, “This is it.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:36.680
This is where he comes back with his 48-caliber
and shoots us all down.”

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:40.780
He even had his hand in his pocket, and of
course he was blocking the only exits.

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:44.500
So then he comes after the people who he had
gotten into it with and they start going at

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:45.500
it some more!

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:49.070
I don't really have any idea what was said
that made this fight so personal that they

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:52.721
still had beef after things had pretty much
already cooled down, and after discussing

00:05:52.721 --> 00:05:53.721
it later, neither of us were able to fully
understand the situation.

00:05:53.721 --> 00:05:55.210
Eventually, after a few more people had fled
the room, they chased the guy out of there

00:05:55.210 --> 00:05:56.990
again and followed him out into the lobby.

00:05:56.990 --> 00:06:00.880
I wish I could have seen what happened out
there, but I also didn't want to make it obvious

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:06.240
that I was watching, and risk getting myself
involved in it.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:10.180
So we were left with just a couple of other
moviegoers by the time this had all died down.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:13.780
We can only assume that someone who had left
has reported the incident at some point.

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:17.680
What was surprising to me is that nobody ever
came back into the theater, except for one

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:21.560
employee who came with a flashlight and collected
something that was left behind.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:25.030
The three goons who had called out​ the
guy on his phone had left their jackets, so

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:29.340
I guess they had the employee guy go collect
them before they they left or got kicked out.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:32.690
Nobody was questioned about the incident and
as far as I know, the police never showed

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:33.690
up.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:36.300
We were not offered a refund, but I'm not
sure if the same held true for the people

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.030
who left, and possibly complained.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:42.170
I was most surprised that the one family who
was sitting down the row from us never returned,

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:45.110
because it seemed like they would just go
get the management and come back.

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:48.590
They also left all of their snacks and stuff
behind, some of which were unopened.

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:52.100
It made me think that something might have
happened to one of them out there, or they

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:55.870
honestly got really scared and just fled the
entire place and never looked back.

00:06:55.870 --> 00:07:00.200
We enjoyed their unopened snacks at the end
as a consolation prize for having sat through

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:04.060
that movie, but we never found out the whole
story of exactly what happened that made the

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:05.280
situation so violent.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:09.240
The scariest thing to me, looking back on
it, is that it easily could have been me who

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:12.920
ended up in the fight, had those other people
not spoken before I did.

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:16.600
The other thing that was kind of haunting
was not knowing what took place outside the

00:07:16.600 --> 00:07:20.080
auditorium, or why none of those other people
ever returned…

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:23.980
Surprisingly enough, this wasn't the only
scary occurrence that I've experienced inside

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:27.860
of a cinema, so join me next time on True
Fear, when I tell the story of what happened

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:49.910
when I went to go see Stephen King’s most
underappreciated movie, 1408.

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:54.490
Remember to subscribe to CZsWorld, for new
horrors every week, punch that deathbell for

00:07:54.490 --> 00:08:04.960
notifications and I'll see you in the next
one.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:21.420
Assuming we both survive.

